+# Philosophy
+
+For whoever proposes a new service or weighs a change that no decision record settles. Larder favors services its
+volunteers can operate with the capacity they have. The same argument came up again in
+[issue 70](../notes/issue-70.md), after session storage and background jobs had already tested the trade-off; future
+proposals start from the tenet below.
+
+## Tenets
+
+### Add a service only for a capability Postgres cannot provide, with two volunteers able to restore it
+
+Larder's baseline is one database and one web app. A proposal to add a service the volunteers must operate has to
+show both what it does that Postgres cannot and which two volunteers can restore it. This rules out adding a
+service merely because it is a familiar tool for the task, or because one volunteer can set it up over a weekend.
+
+In 2023 Redis was proposed for sessions, but only one volunteer would have operated it. Postgres met the need.
+In March 2025 Redis held the Celery queue and evicted 300 receipt jobs on a Saturday night; only one volunteer knew
+how to restore it, and resending receipts took a day of manual work. Postgres also met that need. The decisions to
+[keep sessions in the database](adrs/0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md) and
+[run background jobs on Postgres](adrs/0002-run-background-jobs-on-postgres.md) apply this tenet.
+
+In issue 70, pantry search took four seconds and missed “Rice, brown” when searching for “rice”. Elasticsearch was
+proposed; Postgres full-text search on the item name instead brought the search to 40 ms and found the item.
+That resolved the reported search problem without another service to operate.
+
+External services are allowed: larder already uses a card gateway, and a hosted service that the volunteers do not
+have to run is an option. The rule constrains the services volunteers operate; it does not ban outside capabilities.
